{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And YHWH passed by before him, and YHWH proclaimed: YHWH, YHWH, El, merciful and gracious, long-suffering, and abundant in kindness and truth; keeping kindness unto thousands, forgiving iniquity and transgression and sin; and pardoning.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): these attributes are the attributes of the King toward the people, for they are all included in the attribute of חסד ואמת (kindness and truth). For the King needs to govern his servants and the men of his kingdom with the attribute of kindness — to have mercy upon them and deal with them beyond the strict line of justice — and he also needs to execute justice and to conduct himself at times with the attribute of judgment. This is what Solomon said (Proverbs 20:28): ״חסד ואמת יצרו מלך וסעד בחסד כסאו״ — 'Kindness and truth preserve the king, and his throne is upheld by kindness' — for his kingdom is established through these attributes and they preserve it when he upholds them. And you need to know that whoever understands the thirteen attributes and knows their interpretation and their principles and prays with them with proper intention — his prayer does not return empty, unless he has transgressions in his hand that hold this back. And now, in this time, when we are immersed in exile and have no High Priest to atone for our sins, nor altar to offer sacrifices upon, nor Holy Temple to pray within — nothing remains for us before HaShem except our prayer and His thirteen attributes. And from within these thirteen attributes we have learned the orders of prayer and supplication for mercy from the Master of all, may He be exalted." "<b>And know</b> that the first Name is an essence and not an attribute, and the second Name is an attribute, and it is the attribute of mercy without repentance and without request — rather like a compassionate father who has mercy on his son and knows what he needs and gives to him without his asking. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, has mercy even on the wicked without repentance, as it is written (Psalms 145:9): ״ורחמיו על כל מעשיו״, and even on the gentiles, as the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'the works of My hands are drowning in the sea, and you would say song before Me!' And even on the beasts, as it is said (ibid. 36): ״אדם ובהמה תושיע ה'״. 'El' is the attribute of forgiveness upon request, as Scripture says (ibid. 99): ״אל נושא היית להם״. 'Merciful and gracious' are two attributes of forgiveness upon request together with repentance and suffering, for we find these expressions applying to suffering. Thus it is written (ibid. 78): ״והוא רחום יכפר עון ולא ישחית״ — and this implies that corruption had already begun in him. And further we find (Deuteronomy 4:31): ״כי אל רחום ה' אלהיך לא ירפך ולא ישחיתך״, and we find the expression of destruction in connection with suffering, as it is said (Isaiah 52:14): ״כן משחת מאיש מראהו״, and an explicit verse (Job 33:24): ״ויחננו ויאמר פדעהו מרדת שחת מצאתי כופר״. 'Long-suffering' is an attribute that includes both the righteous and the wicked. And so Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: You must act for the sake of this attribute that is in You, that You are long-suffering toward all. And if You say that they are complete wicked men and unworthy of forbearance but rather liable to destruction — behold, You are 'abundant in kindness,' and You practiced kindness with the twenty-six generations that were from the creation of the world until the giving of the Torah, who had neither Torah nor commandment, and You fed them and sustained them with the attribute of kindness. And if You say that those twenty-six generations did not receive the Torah and did not transgress it, and therefore were not as liable as these who received it and transgressed — You must act for the sake of this attribute that is in You, which is the attribute of truth, that is, kindness of truth that does not anticipate recompense. And if You do not forgive them for the sake of this attribute — behold, You are 'keeping kindness unto thousands,' and You must forgive them in the merit of the Fathers, for You keep kindness unto thousands, and You must preserve for Israel the kindness of the Fathers and their merit. And if You say the merit of the Fathers is exhausted — act for the sake of this attribute that is in You, that You 'forgive iniquity,' and these are the deliberate sins; 'and transgression,' and these are the rebellious acts; 'and sin,' and these are the inadvertent ones; 'and pardoning,' and these are the hidden ones. As it is written (Psalms 19:13): ״מנסתרות נקני״. Now it would have been fitting to precede 'sin' to 'iniquity and transgression,' for if He forgives the deliberate and the rebellious, all the more so the inadvertent. But the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded this to mean that Moses prayed that the deliberate sins be reckoned as inadvertent, and therefore he preceded the deliberate to the inadvertent. And there is another reason: for the sin of the Calf began as inadvertent and ended as deliberate, as I have written above. Therefore Moses did not wish to precede the inadvertent ones, for then his prayer would be a reminder of the sin, and therefore the word וחטאה was written last." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And YHWH passed by before him' — the Holy One, blessed be He, taught Moses how to plead in defense of Israel. He said before Him: Master of the universe, if they had no merit You would have destroyed them; remember Abraham, Isaac, and Israel Your servants. 'YHWH, YHWH, El, merciful and gracious, long-suffering' — for the righteous and for the wicked. Moses said to Him: the wicked should perish! The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: By your life, you will have need of this. When the matter of the spies occurred, Moses said (Numbers 14:17–18): ״ועתה יגדל נא כח ה' כאשר דברת לאמר ה' ארך אפים״ — 'long-suffering' both for the righteous and for the wicked." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to ask concerning what Onkelos translated: ארך אפים — 'מרחיק רגז' (He distances wrath) — for it alludes to the angels appointed to destroy, who are distant from the chamber of the Holy One, blessed be He." "<b>And likewise</b> you will find in the Jerusalemite Talmud, in tractate Ta'anit: Rabbi Levi said: What is ארך אפים מרחיק רגז? A parable of a king who had two fierce legions. The king said: If they are stationed with me in the city, the townspeople anger me and they come and destroy them. Rather, I will dispatch them to a distant road, so that if the townspeople anger me, by the time I send for them the townspeople will have appeased me. So said the Holy One, blessed be He: 'Wrath' and 'Fury' are two angels of destruction. If they are stationed with Me, now Israel anger Me and they come and destroy them. Rather, I will dispatch them to a distant road, so that if Israel anger Me, by the time I send for them they do repentance and I accept them. What is the reason? As it is said (Isaiah 13:5): ״באים מארץ מרחק מקצה השמים ה' וכלי זעמו״. Rabbi Yitzchak said: And not only that, but He locks before them — what is the reason? As it is said (Jeremiah 50:25): ״פתח ה' את אוצרו ויוצא את כלי זעמו״ — 'עד די פתח' (until He opened) — 'עד די טריף רחמוהי קריבין' (until the time that His mercies are drawn near). It was taught in the name of Rabbi Meir: 'Behold HaShem goes forth from His place' — He goes forth from one attribute to another; He goes forth from the attribute of justice and comes to the attribute of mercy. Thus far the Jerusalemite Talmud. And the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded in the Sifre: a covenant was sealed for the thirteen attributes, that they do not return empty, as it is said: ״הנה אנכי כורת ברית״. And if transgressions multiply, He reverses His attributes — as it is written (Isaiah 1:4): ״עזבו את ה' נאצו את קדוש ישראל נזורו אחור״. What is 'עזבו את ה'? The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I called Myself ה', El, merciful and gracious, and now because of your transgressions I have become cruel, and you have reversed My attributes — this is what is written: נזורו אחור — the attributes were turned backward." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): 'And YHWH passed by before him, and proclaimed: YHWH, YHWH, El' — here are three names, and the rest are attributes, and they are ten. The attributes are predicated of the names: the three names correspond to the three worlds — the world of angels, the world of the spheres, and the lower world. And corresponding to the two worlds that are existent, eternal, and unchanging, He mentioned two names that do not change, and they are similar to one another, just as those two worlds are similar to one another in respect of existence and eternity; as David said, peace be upon him (Psalms 148:5–6): 'כי הוא צוה ונבראו ויעמידם לעד לעולם' — and He mentioned them above, saying (ibid. 148:1–2): 'הללוהו במרומים הללוהו כל מלאכיו.' And corresponding to this lower world, which is subject to change and possesses becoming and perishing, He mentioned El, which is a name of attribute, and which is transformed from the attribute of justice to the attribute of mercy; as it says (ibid. 22): 'אלי אלי למה עזבתני' — and the third [word] is an attribute of mercy in this place, for one does not say to the attribute of justice 'why have You forsaken me.' There remain ten attributes by which the Creator, may He be exalted, governs His world. רחום וחנון — two attributes are stated concerning providence, general and particular: רחום is a general attribute, as it says (ibid. 145:9): 'ורחמיו על כל מעשיו'; and חנון is a particular attribute, and this attribute is found only in the matter of man, as it is written (Job 33:24): 'ויחננו ויאמר פדעהו' etc., and written (Genesis 43:29): 'אלהים יחנך בני'; and because man is the most honored of all the creatures of the lower world, He singled him out with a distinct attribute regarding providence. ארך אפים — this is an attribute that all need, both righteous and wicked, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:20): 'כי אדם אין צדיק בארץ אשר יעשה טוב ולא יחטא' — and wicked men, needless to say; and this attribute is found only in the species of man alone, for it pertains to the providence of punishment and reward. There remain seven attributes: three for the good and four for the wicked. רב חסד ואמת and נוצר חסד לאלפים — for the good; נושא עון ופשע וחטאה ונקה — for the wicked. נושא עון is the sin of thought — עון is the straying of the heart; פשע וחטאה is the sin of deed — one deliberate and one inadvertent; and נקה — our Sages, of blessed memory, said: ונקה לשבים, for it is impossible that the Creator, may He be exalted, would acquit those who do not repent, for it is not fitting to forgive a wicked person who stands in his rebellion, for if so we find a sinner rewarded, and Gehenna and its punishments were created only for his sake, and punishment and reward would be nullified." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'And YHWH passed by before him, and proclaimed: YHWH, YHWH, El, רחום וחנון, ארך אפים ורב חסד ואמת, נוצר חסד לאלפים, נושא עון ופשע וחטאה ונקה' — all these thirteen attributes are drawn from the Keter, for the term מדות [means] vessels or recipients. And this is the order of the reading of the attributes among the sefirot: ה' ה' — Hokhmah and Binah; אל רחום — Gedulah; וחנון ארך אפים — Gevurah; ורב חסד ואמת — Tiferet; נוצר חסד נושא עון — Netzah; ופשע וחטאה — Hod; ונקה — Yesod: for each sefirah two attributes, except for Yesod, which has only one among the attributes. And the august Name that passed by before him — He is the One who calls these thirteen attributes that are drawn from the Keter." "<b>But</b> the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory, and his intention regarding the thirteen attributes is: the first ה' is El Elyon, and the second and third are Tiferet and Kavod, and all three of them are the very essence of the Name; and afterward, ten attributes. רחום וחנון ארך אפים belong to El Elyon — therefore He did not say מרחם מחונן ומאריך, for He is the very essence active in those attributes. And He is רב חסד ואמת נוצר חסד in His Tiferet, and נושא עון ופשע וחטאה ונקה in His Kavod. And know that between the first Name and the second there is a pause between them, and anyone who did not pause is destined to give account." "<b>And furthermore,</b> regarding the thirteen attributes there is another approach among some masters of the Kabbalah: all the attributes together are arranged in fours — that is, after this first, supreme Name, in which the attributes are unified and which is itself not an attribute, there are found twelve attributes, and they are organized in fours. He first mentioned four attributes for the attribute of mercy; after that he mentioned four attributes for the attribute of חסד; and after that four attributes for the attribute of judgment. Now this is along the lines of the three sefirot, which are the three patriarchs, about whom the masters of truth said: they themselves are the Chariot. From here you shall understand that Moses, when he praised HaShem with three praises — namely, when he said 'האל הגדול הגבור והנורא' — included all thirteen of these attributes within those three: for the 'El' that he mentioned corresponds to the first ה', which is essence and not an attribute; and when he mentioned 'הגדול,' he included in it four attributes of חסד; and when he mentioned 'הגבור,' he included in it four attributes of the attribute of judgment; and when he mentioned 'הנורא,' he included in it four attributes of the attribute of mercy. Thus with these three praises of Moses they are all thirteen attributes that were stated to him at Sinai. And because the One who passed before the face of Moses is the 'Good' that was promised above — 'אני אעביר כל טובי' — and He is the One who calls the thirteen attributes, as I have already mentioned, therefore you will find in a Torah scroll tagin on the Name of 'ויעבור ה' על פניו': two tagin on the first ה and two tagin on the last ה; but in the two Names, which are ה' ה', there are no tagin at all. And it is known that the tagin, wherever they appear, are an allusion to apprehension and contemplation. Now, the apprehension is truly possible regarding the One who passes and calls, but not regarding Him to whom we attribute the attributes — for in Him the power of apprehension fails. And understand this." "<b>And</b> our master Rav Hai Gaon, of blessed memory, wrote that these thirteen attributes are derivative offshoots drawn from the ten sefirot, and even though these are called מדות and those ספירות, all flows from one spring. And even though we find thirteen attributes and do not find thirteen sefirot, the secret of the matter is that the three supreme lights, which are above the ten sefirot, have no beginning, for they are Name and essence to the root of all roots. And the tradition of the Gaon regarding their names is: אור קדמון, אור מצוחצח, אור צח — and all three of these names are one matter and one essence, bound with a mighty bond to the root of all roots; and from the root of all roots the ten sefirot were emanated; and regarding this the masters of truth alluded to 'the thorn of the יו״ד' — and I have already mentioned this in the section of Bereshit. And may the Holy One, blessed be He, forgive us and show us wonders in His Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {4 "The Aramaic phrases 'עד די פתח' and 'עד די טריף רחמוהי קריבין' are reproduced as they appear in the Yerushalmi citation; the first appears to play on the Hebrew of Jeremiah 50:25, the second is Aramaic for 'until His mercies are drawn near.'", 5 "The closing words of this comment flow directly into a statement from the Sages about ונקה; the argument trails off mid-flow as is characteristic of Rabbeinu Bahya's prose leading into the next comment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:46.777579505Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}